{align:justify|One July morning in 1978, on a plateau in Taipei County, Thai A Vuong and his wife, together with their close friend Lam Thuan Xa, shouldered carrying poles up the mountain to pick lychees. Passing a long-abandoned cave, they suddenly saw three dogs dash out, their jaws dripping with human entrails still oozing blood. Curious, Mr. Xa crept inside to see what was happening — but the dreadful sight rooted him in horror: a gentleman over sixty lay in a pool of blood, chest and belly torn open, his innards carried off by those same three dogs. Madder still, sitting beside the corpse was a disheveled mad girl, playfully winding the victim's intestines around her neck.}
{align:justify|Mr. Xa's hair stood on end with fright; he spun around, fled the cave, and at once went with the Vuongs to report the crime.}
{align:justify|Police rushed to the scene. Apart from the hollowed-out chest and belly, the victim bore no other wounds. The puzzle: why was the mad girl beside him unharmed? Why had the dogs bitten the old man but not her?...}
{align:justify|Question upon question arose, and the mystery of the case seemed to defy the squad's professional skill.}
{align:justify|With the public's helpful cooperation, police swiftly learned the dead man was Lieu Ky Duc, and the mad girl Lieu Tu Tu — his daughter, now gravely mentally ill. They lived nearby in Shou Lin township.}
{align:justify|Records showed that in 1949 Mr. Duc had followed the Nationalist government to Taiwan, serving 28 years as a judge. Learned in law but crooked at heart, greedy for wealth and lustful to the extreme, he was a model corrupt official, retired a year before to live hidden in Shou Lin. A few of his deeds will suffice:}
{align:justify|In 1953, a Taipei customs official named Nguu Dong Doan, convicted of graft, was sentenced to ten years by the court of first instance. At retrial, the former judge having been transferred, Mr. Duc was appointed to hear the case.}
{align:justify|In court, seeing Mr. Doan's mistress Phan Chau in the gallery — a woman of striking beauty — Judge Duc stared without pause. He then deliberately blustered at Mr. Doan, threatening that ten years was far too light, and if new evidence emerged he would give at least fifteen!}
{align:justify|Terrified, Mr. Doan clasped his hands and begged the judge for leniency. But Mr. Duc refused.}
{align:justify|Days later Mr. Duc personally telephoned Mr. Doan's defense lawyer for a private meeting, hinting darkly that the sentence could be heavy or light — if the beauty Phan Chau would plead in person, perhaps it could be changed...}
{align:justify|The lawyer saw through Mr. Duc at once and promised to persuade Mr. Doan to let Phan Chau attend upon Mr. Duc for a week. Even then, Mr. Duc was unsatisfied and forced Mr. Doan's family to pay an enormous sum. Only when beauty and gold were both harvested to his liking did Mr. Duc declare in open court that evidence was insufficient, sentencing Mr. Doan to six months — convertible to a fine instead of jail. In effect, acquittal: Mr. Duc single-handedly freed the prisoner and covered the sky with one hand!}
{align:justify|Mr. Duc had come to Taiwan alone; after only a few years as judge, graft made him hugely rich. Through friends he married Thuy Dao, a farmer's daughter thirteen years his junior — gentle and lovely. The next year little Tu Tu was born.}
{align:justify|Sadly, when Tu Tu was two, Thuy Dao died of breast cancer. Busy with "official duties," Mr. Duc neglected the child. Once, when a cold brought a raging fever, she was not taken to hospital in time and became gravely simple-minded.}
{align:justify|His wife's death and his daughter's imbecility were two heavy blows. Yet Mr. Duc never once reflected that these were retribution for his lack of virtue. Instead he exploited his office all the harder to seize fame and profit and stuff his private coffers — even boasting loudly that with his power, rank, talent, and scheming, countless beauties would come offering themselves and gold and silver would pour into his pockets by themselves!...}
{align:justify|So through 28 years in power, day after day, month after month, year after year, he did nothing but outrage heaven and reason: extorting and blackmailing by every trick, manufacturing countless wrongful convictions and fatal cases.}
{align:justify|Many, wrongfully sentenced for his bribes, died of grief and sickness in prison while their wives killed themselves in protest. How many families were shattered because a man of high office had no conscience?}
{align:justify|During his judgeship, how many lives did he ruin, how many families destroy? His crimes piled mountain-high...}
{align:justify|In 1977 Mr. Duc finally retired, living hidden with a widow on the Shou Lin plateau.}
{align:justify|One morning, passing the cave with his daughter on the way to a festival, Tu Tu asked to rest inside a while. Indulging her, he stepped in — when suddenly three dogs charged at him, ferocious, baring sharp fangs and raking his chest and ripping his belly with savage claws. Within minutes they had dragged out all his vital organs, and Mr. Duc died in uttermost agony.}
{align:justify|The three "executioner" dogs carried out Mr. Duc's death sentence swiftly. Mission done, they vanished like lightning without a trace. Only Tu Tu sat beside the body, beaming with joy, calmly winding her father's intestines about her neck at play, looking as pleased as if she had just done something very fine.}
{align:justify|A judge's office is to uphold the law justly, enforcing the nation's statutes without fear or favor! But Mr. Duc, besotted with lust and loot, abused his power wholly for personal gain, betraying the trust of nation and people.}
{align:justify|His immediate retribution stands as a warning to the world: though you may dodge the punishment of human law, you can never dodge the retribution of cause and effect. As the saying goes: "Heaven's justice is utterly fair — you may hide for a while, but not for a lifetime."}
